WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.750
Black holes. We're riveted by their
destructive power.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.930
Black holes are dangerous.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.640
Black holes are hazards.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.610
Black holes are not friendly for their
environments.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.670
There's just no good end to anything that
falls into a black hole.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.540
Perhaps one of the most frightening objects
in the universe.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.660
But what exactly are these scary objects?

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.310
Black holes are created when you get enough
matter in a small region of space.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.830
This happens when a massive star dies and
collapses in on itself.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:45.090
A supernova.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.790
A black hole is the ultimate consequence of
gravity.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:58.030
It's an object that has so much mass crushed
into such a small space that its escape

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.860
velocity becomes greater than the speed of
light.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.510
They are a one way street.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:09.840
You go in.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.330
Nothing escapes.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.440
Not even light.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.330
But do black holes really deserve their bad
rap?

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.040
In some ways I think we set up black holes to
be more villains than they actually are.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:26.410
Black holes suffer a bit of a PR problem.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:31.360
I think there are a lot more menacing in
science fiction and popular media than they

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.320
really are.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.140
There are trillions of galaxies in the known
universe.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.410
And most of them have a supermassive black
hole at their center.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.990
These monsters are millions of times the
mass of our sun.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:00.310
Their immense gravity can send stars flying.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:05.640
They're instrumental in choreographing the
dance of stars in their vicinity.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:14.690
Supermassive black holes shoot out torrents
of lethal radiation and violent cosmic winds.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.310
And gobble up anything that comes close.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:27.150
Now scientists are beginning to realize
these cosmic giants may also have a creative

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:27.660
side.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.930
Most people think of black holes as being
like giant vacuum cleaners in space, and

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.580
basically everything falls into them.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.050
But that's that's not actually the case.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:41.760
They're better thought of as the engines of
cosmic change.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:49.860
Although black holes are the end states of
stars, they can actually influence the

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.850
formation of stars as well in a bunch of
different ways.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:02.010
A galaxy's job is to make stars, but
uncontrolled star growth isn't healthy.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.610
Too many stars can drain a galaxy's gas
supply.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.950
Black holes are very important.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:15.280
It appears that Galaxy evolution is tied to
black hole evolution.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:21.390
We don't know exactly how yet, but the
marriage appears certain.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:26.490
One idea is that supermassive black holes.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.360
Act as cosmic control mechanisms.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.930
Black holes can act like a thermostat in your
house.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:38.910
If your house gets too hot, the thermostat
will kick on the air conditioner.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.160
And if it gets too cold, it'll kick on the
heater.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.910
Black holes do the same things for galaxies.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:52.180
Supermassive black holes regulate star
formation by pulling gas in and shooting it

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.160
back out into the galaxy.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:59.900
When these black holes are consuming matter,
they're drawing matter into themselves, but

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.180
they're also spewing stuff out.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:07.700
Basically black holes eat like little babies,
very sloppily.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.090
So a lot of what they eat comes flying back
out again.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:17.210
They stars, they eat planets, but most often
they eat giant clouds of gas.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:22.340
The black hole drags gas and dust into an
accretion disk around it.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:26.180
This disk spins faster and faster.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.550
Magnetic energy builds up.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:32.870
With the accretion disk swirling around the
black hole.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.420
There are also magnetic fields that are
going on.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:40.520
The material is moving so rapidly that the
magnetic field sort of winds up, coils up and

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.470
forms a vortex like a tornado.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.150
Astronomers call them jets.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:55.530
These jets propagate outward, like freight
trains plowing through the galaxy over

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.630
hundreds and thousands of light years.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.260
These are like death rays.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:08.050
The Jets disrupt the star forming gas clouds,
limiting excess star formation in the main

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:09.340
body of the galaxy.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.550
But in the very outer reaches of the galaxy,
they can spark star birth.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.250
Things are more gentle out there.

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.380
You're not as close to the energetic heart.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.480
So stars, planets and life can form out
there, partially because of the material that

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.310
the black hole has moved out there.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:36.610
So black holes can have outsized influence on
the regions that they inhabit right around

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:42.880
them. They can prevent the formation of
stars, whereas on very, very large scales,

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.000
they can actually instigate the formation of
stars.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:54.650
2018. Black holes hit the front page.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:05.650
Scientists discovered black holes gobbling
up gas so fast that they seem to be

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:08.050
outgrowing their host galaxies.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:14.460
It naturally makes the question come up How
big can a black hole get?

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:17.600
Now we have the answer.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:24.620
They can reach size Triple XL becoming
ultramassive black holes.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:45.390
Ultramassive black holes are so cool because
it's just mind boggling that black holes so

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:46.860
large can exist.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:53.280
Ultramassive Black holes are very rare and
typically have masses of more than 10 billion

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:54.960
times the mass of the sun.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:58.100
10 billion solar masses.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:02.010
That's a ten followed by nine zeros.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.460
Ultramassive Black holes are real beasts.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:10.080
The black hole at the center of our galaxy
is 4 million solar masses.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:13.680
Imagine black holes that are 2500 times
bigger.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.720
That's what we're talking about here.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:25.330
An ultra massive black hole this big would be
as wide as the solar system.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:31.590
And weigh as much as all the stars in the
Milky Way.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:37.120
There are inside galaxies that aren't a whole
lot bigger.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:39.160
That really surprised the hell out of
everybody.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:50.170
And in 2018, scientists discover a 20 billion
solar mass ultramassive black hole growing

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:52.720
faster than any other black hole.

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:01.320
This ravenous behemoth devours the mass of
our sun every two days.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:08.130
These big black holes are really good at
gobbling up other things.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:09.960
They literally eat anything.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.670
They're monsters of the universe.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:17.330
This kind of voracious eating can have
devastating consequences.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:25.360
It blasts so much energy and turbulence into
the galaxy that stars no longer form.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.890
And the bigger the black hole, the faster
the galaxy dies.

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:36.710
The primary thing these ultramassive black
holes do to galaxies is they shut down all

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:40.130
star formation. And so in that sense, they
kind of kill galaxies.

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:44.510
And so these things could even wipe out their
host galaxies.

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:50.000
Ultramassive black holes are a problem for
scientists to.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.570
They might be the fastest eaters.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:58.050
But that doesn't explain how they got so
large.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.770
With these ultramassive black holes, these
black holes that are tens of billions of

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.270
times more massive than our sun.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:10.110
You can't just grow them from the slow
accretion of gas over time.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.170
There's just not enough gas and there's just
not enough time.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.750
It gives us a new mystery to solve.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.240
How do you make black holes that are just
that big?

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:25.920
There's not a clear answer so far as to how
these ultramassive black holes were formed.

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:30.210
People wonder if there some other mechanism
by which you could make black holes.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:36.870
A mechanism so violent it also throws
supermassive black holes clean out of

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:37.830
galaxies.

